In "reply all / reply to list" [1], Pat asked that replies from
process-email follow mailing list convention and reply to all. Until
now every reply, and every series, went to Pat alone, so the list
never saw the answers or the patches.

Reply-all is the norm on the lists we resemble. The Linux kernel says
to "always reply-all when composing a response, unless you have a
good reason to do otherwise" [2]. Git asks to "respond to them with
Reply-All on the mailing list" and sends patches To the maintainer
with the list on Cc [3]. PostgreSQL [4] and FreeBSD [5] say the same.
Debian is the exception: it asks you not to Cc the original poster
unless they request it [6].

Patch 1 makes the reply a reply-all and adds the list to Cc when the
message being answered left it off. Patch 2 copies the list on each
series.

notmuch reply --reply-to=all puts the sender and the original To
addresses together in To. For a message to lab-devel@p7x.dev, that
gives "To: Pat, lab-devel@p7x.dev" rather than the list in To and
Pat on Cc. Either way, everyone gets the reply. I left the notmuch
layout as it is rather than rewriting headers by hand.

The series touches the same file as "[PATCH 0/3] Archive messages
that need no answer" [7], but not the same lines. The two apply in
either order without conflict.

